Fix colour sampling of images in the test device

Carried locally against mupdf 1.28.2. Not submitted upstream.

Three defects in the image sampling paths of fz_test_fill_image(),
fz_test_fill_compressed_8bpc_image() and fz_test_fill_other_image() made colour
detection read uninitialised memory or undecoded data. For ProFile Counter that
meant a page could be classified black-and-white or colour at random between
runs, and greyscale images in several formats were always reported as colour.

1. The colour converter is declared as

       void (*convert)(fz_context *, fz_color_converter *, const float *src, float *dst)

   and every other caller in the library passes (src, dst). Both call sites here
   pass (dst, src): the uninitialised ds[] is handed over as the source, the
   converted result is written into cs[] (which held the real pixel data), and
   the code then tests ds[], which is still uninitialised stack memory. Pass the
   arrays in the declared order.

2. fz_test_fill_other_image() computes the per-pixel component count as
   pix->n - 1, unconditionally assuming an alpha channel. For a pixmap without
   alpha the count becomes 0, so the sampling loop never reads the pixmap and
   leaves cs[] unwritten. Subtract the alpha channel only when there is one, and
   bail out rather than sample if a pixmap reports no colour components at all.

3. fz_test_fill_image() takes a fast path that samples the image's compressed
   buffer directly, but fz_open_compressed_buffer() only decodes the sample
   oriented compressions. The "full image formats" -- JPX, PNG, TIFF, BMP, GIF,
   PNM, PSD, JXR -- fall through the switch in fz_open_image_decomp_stream() and
   are returned still encoded, so the sampling loop reads compressed bytes as if
   they were 8-bit pixels. Whatever the header and entropy coded data happen to
   contain decides the verdict, and because that data is effectively random no
   threshold below 1 suppresses it.

   JPX shows it most clearly because its magic number is fixed: offset 3 of the
   JP2 signature box is 0C 6A 50, a channel spread of 94, so detection trips on
   the ASCII "jP" at the second pixel before anything has been decoded.

   The reach is wider than JPX. Inside a PDF only JPXDecode takes this path, but
   pfc-tool also opens standalone images, where BMP, PNG and PNM files arrive
   here directly. Measured against the previously shipped build over the full
   corpus, a 1bpp black-and-white BMP scan, a greyscale PNG and a greyscale BMP
   were each reported as colour; all three render with a maximum per-pixel
   channel spread of 0 and are correctly black-and-white after this change.

   Restrict the fast path to the compressions that really do decode to samples
   and let everything else go through fz_get_pixmap_from_image().

Defect 1 is the one that affects ordinary ICC and Flate encoded images and is a
regression against the 1.6 series this project previously used, which was
deterministic on the same files. Defect 3 is independent of the other two: it
lives in the fz_device_rgb branch, which uses is_rgb_color_u8() and no colour
converter at all.

To refresh after a mupdf version bump: apply by hand, adjust, then regenerate
with `git diff -- source/fitz/test-device.c` from within the submodule.

